LSM trees favor write-heavy workloads because many random logical updates become sequential segment writes, at the price of read amplification and background-compaction I/O. A Bloom-filter false positive only causes an unnecessary segment read; a false negative would hide real data, so the implementation inserts tombstones as well as live keys and treats negative results as the only definitive result. This lab compacts every segment, but leveled or tiered production compaction may cover only part of the key space, so it must retain a tombstone until no older overlapping file can still contain the deleted value. A write-ahead log, block indexes, checksums, manifests, snapshots, and rate-limited compaction are also required before this design is durable production storage.
